{
  "name": "Schrödinger tunnelling",
  "description": "Linear Schrödinger equation: two identical packets, each with energy about 2, hit barriers of height V = 3, more than they have, so classically both bounce straight back. The top barrier is thin (2 cells): a fair part of the wave tunnels through. The bottom one is three times as thick (6 cells): tunnelling falls off exponentially with thickness and almost nothing gets through. The lanes are separated by a wall; the border absorbs. Try: lower a barrier's V below 2 and most of the packet passes over it.",
